-- title:
--   Serre's theorem A in the charts of an affine morphism to P^N_A
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring, $N$ a natural number and $X$ a scheme, and let $\varphi : X \to \operatorname{Proj}$ of the graded ring $A[x_0,\dots,x_N]$ (taken with its homogeneous submodule grading) be an affine morphism. Let $\pi : X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ be a morphism and let $F$ be an `OModulePresheaf` over $\pi$: an assignment to each open $U \subseteq X$ of a module over both $A$ and $\Gamma(X,U)$ (compatibly), together with $A$-linear restriction maps that are semilinear for the restriction of functions and functorial. Assume $F$ is coherent, i.e. $F(U)$ is a finite $\Gamma(X,U)$-module for every affine open $U$, and quasi-coherent, i.e. for every affine open $U$ and every $f \in \Gamma(X,U)$ each element of $F(X_f)$ becomes, after multiplication by some power of $f$, the restriction of an element of $F(U)$, and each element of $F(U)$ restricting to $0$ on $X_f$ is annihilated by some power of $f$. Then there is $d_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for every $d \ge d_0$ there are $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and families $s_a = (s_{a,i})_{i \in \mathrm{Fin}(N+1)}$, $a \in \mathrm{Fin}(m)$, with $s_{a,i} \in F(U_i)$ for the charts $U_i = \varphi^{-1}D_+(x_i)$, such that on the overlap of $U_i$ and $U_j$ (restrictions taken along `ProjSpace.pullbackOverlap_le_left φ i j` and `ProjSpace.pullbackOverlap_le_right φ i j`) one has $s_{a,i}| = (u_{ij}|)^d \cdot s_{a,j}|$, where $u_{ij} = \varphi^\sharp(x_j/x_i) \in \Gamma(X,U_i)$, and such that for every $i$ the elements $s_{1,i},\dots,s_{m,i}$ span $F(U_i)$ as a $\Gamma(X,U_i)$-module.
--
--   This is Serre's theorem A in chart form: a family satisfying the stated transition rule is a global section of the twist $F(d) = F \otimes \varphi^*\mathcal{O}(d)$ expressed in the frames $x_i^d$, so the conclusion says that $F(d)$ is generated by finitely many global sections for all large $d$. It is used downstream to produce global sections of twists and to realise $F$ as the sheaf attached to a finitely generated graded module.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_OModulePresheaf_exists_forall_exists_res_eq_frameUnit_pow_smul_res_and_span_eq_top.lean

